sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1 (an external API call is an effect, so it is a run), R2-R4. docs/reference/conn.md#native-pairing-revocation.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rule 16 (the session list only picks the targets, so it folds into the revoke step).
  %% status: migrated
  %% command: none · API (conn-pairing-revoke)
  %% kind: conn.pairing_revoke
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane T3 icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>API callers
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant T3 as T3 server<br/>T3 auth API
  CLI->>GW: Revoke own pairings<br/>POST …/env-beast/pairings/revoke
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `POST /api/v1/conn/environments/env-beast/pairings/revoke`
  %%> - syntax: the MCP tool `conn-pairing-revoke` with `environment` and an optional `node_id`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the T3 server, and the Node whose pairings go; without `node_id` it is the caller
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - there is no `orbit` command; WireGuard access does not change
  opt node_id (API)
    Note over GW: Select another Node<br/>node_id: 4
    %%> Input:
    %%> - `node_id` selects Node 4 instead of the caller
    %%> - the caller remains the owner of the revocation Operation
  end
  Note over GW: Select the pairings<br/>task #5171 · session to vault
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the `conn` extension is on; the caller is an active Node
  %%> - the caller has node access to the Node that registered env-beast, else `node_access.required`
  %%> - env-beast is registered and the Gateway's admin session there has not expired
  %%> - no other revocation runs for env-beast; the Gateway holds one lock per server
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - captures the subject Node's open pairings on env-beast: their client labels and link IDs; the subject defaults to the caller
  %%> - pushes the admin session to the Swamp vault as `conn-session-5171` after commit (R3)
  %%> Why a run:
  %%> - calling T3's API is an effect; the Gateway's only execution primitives are mint and sign
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-conn-pairing-revoke
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); no Node is a target
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the T3 server URL, the captured labels and link IDs, and the vault key names; never the session
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5171
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the caller waits on the task and shows its progress
  Note over SW: Create private replies
  %%> How:
  %%> - mktemp creates a private worker directory; no raw HTTP reply enters run history
  SW->>T3: Read session inventory
  %%> How:
  %%> - one bounded curl request with the admin session from vault.get
  %%> - no read is needed when no admitted label exists
  Note over SW: Select the sessions
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - typed data selects admitted labels and keeps the current admin and every other label
  %%> - an invalid or failed list refuses before any revoke
  SW->>T3: Revoke the sessions
  %%> How:
  %%> - one bounded curl request per selected session, in native forEach
  %%> - the reply stays in the private directory; no duplicate ownership read
  Note over SW: Read session revocations
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - typed data reads the private replies and keeps valid completions and the revoked count
  SW->>T3: Revoke unused links
  %%> How:
  %%> - one bounded curl request per captured link; a failed session phase blocks these calls
  Note over SW: Read link revocations
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - typed data keeps each valid link completion, including partial failure
  Note over SW: Remove private replies
  %%> How:
  %%> - declared completed or skipped dependencies remove the directory after failure too
  Note over SW: Confirm revocation
  %%> Check:
  %%> - the native assert requires valid replies for every admitted action
  alt every revoke done
    T3-->>SW: 2 sessions revoked
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - each revoke answered
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · revoked
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Mark pairings revoked<br/>revoked_at · task done
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the subject Node's pairings on env-beast get `revoked_at`, and their stored links are removed
    %%> - the device loses env-beast at once; its other servers and Orbit access stay
    %%> - task #5171 succeeds
  else listing or a revoke fails
    T3--xSW: 401 or no answer
    %%> When:
    %%> - T3 refuses the admin session, cannot be reached, or answers with invalid data
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>conn.revocation_failed
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its safe error code
    Note over GW: Mark revoked ones only<br/>task failed · rerun is safe
    %%> Result:
    %%> - pairings whose sessions and links completed are marked revoked; the rest stay open; task #5171 fails
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - safe: run it again; it lists the sessions again and skips those already gone
  end
  loop wait with progress until terminal
    CLI->>GW: Read Operation #5171<br/>GET conn/operations/5171
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - waits on the task and shows each step label as progress
    GW-->>CLI: progress + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the progress, and the number of revoked sessions once the task has ended
  end
